{
  "description": "Futures account history paging (GET /api/v1/futures/fills and /api/v1/futures/funding), for an SDK's iterate-all helper. Rules: (1) the cursor is opaque text, sent back exactly as received (percent-encoded per RFC 3986 in the query; the server decodes it to the same text); (2) a page may be short, even empty, and still carry a next_cursor: keep paging until next_cursor is null; (3) an EMPTY page whose next_cursor equals the cursor just sent means the provider is busy: wait (the SDK's normal backoff) and request the same cursor again, at most max_busy_retries times (default 3) in a row; this is a setting of its own, independent of the client's request retry count (a client with retries disabled must still ride out a busy provider), then fail with a retryable error (code PAGING_STALLED, a local error) without returning a partial result as complete; (4) a NON-empty page whose next_cursor equals the cursor just sent is a server error: yield its rows, then fail with a local error code PAGING_CURSOR_REPEATED, not retryable (never loop); (5) has_account false ends iteration with no rows; (6) the first request sends no cursor. Each case lists the pages in order: `request_cursor` is what the SDK must send (null = parameter absent), `response` what the server answers. `expect.ids` are the yielded row ids in order. (7) a retryable error mid-paging (e.g. 503 SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE with Retry-After) is retried with the client's normal request-retry policy for the SAME cursor and paging continues; a page entry with `http_status` is an error answer whose body is `response` and whose headers are `headers`. Real cursors look like \"<ms>.<key>\" or a bare \"<ms>\": still opaque. `funding_cases` repeat the rules on GET /api/v1/futures/funding, whose rows have no id: `expect.times` lists the yielded rows' `time`.",
